oneplus-erhai-mesa
==================

Payload lives entirely under /usr -- no ld.so.conf.d entry, no
alternatives, no diversions. That is deliberate: putting these libraries on the
default loader path would replace the system Mesa for every process on the
machine, including ones that are happier on llvmpipe.

To use it in a session, point the loader and the ICD/EGL registries at it:

    LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/usr/lib/aarch64-linux-gnu
    VK_DRIVER_FILES=/usr/share/vulkan/icd.d/freedreno_icd.aarch64.json
    VK_ICD_FILENAMES=/usr/share/vulkan/icd.d/freedreno_icd.aarch64.json
    __EGL_VENDOR_LIBRARY_FILENAMES=/usr/share/glvnd/egl_vendor.d/50_mesa.json
    GALLIUM_DRIVER=freedreno      # or MESA_LOADER_DRIVER_OVERRIDE=zink

/usr/share/oneplus-erhai-mesa/10-desktop.conf.gpu is exactly that, in
systemd environment.d format. erhai-desktop-gl.service copies it into the
desktop user's ~/.config/environment.d/ when /proc/device-tree says the GPU
node is enabled, and copies the llvmpipe variant when it is not -- a session
that forces freedreno against a disabled GPU dies with "No GPUs found" and
bounces gdm back to the greeter.

environment.d is read once, when the user manager starts, so changes need a
reboot (restarting gdm is not enough -- the user manager survives it).

Notes
-----
* Native gallium is roughly 4x faster than zink here (glmark2-wayland ~11000 vs
  ~2700), so it is the default. Blender is smoother on zink; launch it with
  MESA_LOADER_DRIVER_OVERRIDE=zink (that variable wins over an inherited
  GALLIUM_DRIVER=freedreno; setting GALLIUM_DRIVER=zink does not).
* Never switch the compositor's GL driver on a live session -- change the env
  and reboot. Switching live leaves two gnome-shells fighting over the GPU and
  looks exactly like a driver hang.
* If the system mesa-vulkan-drivers package is installed, GTK4's Vulkan
  renderer may pick up the stale system turnip instead of this one.
